// Rich fallback markdown documentation for BlackParrot and BOOM
const blackParrotFallbackReadme = `
# BlackParrot: Modular Coherent RV64GC Multicore Processor

BlackParrot is an open-source, Linux-capable, cache-coherent RISC-V multicore processor developed jointly by the University of Washington and Boston University. Designed with a strict "tiny, trusty, and timely" philosophy, BlackParrot aims to be the default open-source silicon standard for academic and industrial research.

## Microarchitecture Highlights
- **Pipeline:** 5-stage in-order frontend with dual-issue dispatch, decoupled backend execution units, and out-of-order writeback for multi-cycle operations.
- **ISA Support:** RV64GC (RV64IMAFDC) complying with RISC-V Privileged Architecture v1.11, supporting User (U), Supervisor (S), and Machine (M) modes.
- **Virtual Memory:** Hardware Sv39 MMU with configurable multi-entry translation lookaside buffers (ITLB and DTLB) and hardware page table walkers (PTW).
- **Coherence Protocol:** Directory-based MESI cache coherence protocol operating over the open BedRock interconnect standard, formally verified using CMurphi model checking against safety and deadlock invariants.
- **Cache Subsystem:** Split 32 KB 8-way set-associative L1 instruction and data caches with non-blocking miss handling via Miss Status Holding Registers (MSHRs).

## Silicon Validation & Tapeouts
- **TSMC 12nm FFC:** Quad-core BlackParrot cluster taped out in 2019 running at >1 GHz.
- **TSMC 12nm Dual-Core:** Second-generation dual-core testchip taped out in 2020.
- **GlobalFoundries 28nm:** Tapeout validation campaigns in 2022 and 2025 demonstrating full multi-user Linux boots.

## Verification & FPGA Emulation
- **CMurphi:** Automated formal verification of cache coherence state transitions.
- **ZynqParrot:** Turnkey FPGA emulation environment mapping full BlackParrot clusters onto Xilinx Zynq-7000 and UltraScale+ platforms with real serial console and SD card storage.
`;

const boomFallbackReadme = `
# SonicBOOM: Berkeley Out-of-Order RISC-V Machine

The Berkeley Out-of-Order Machine (BOOM) is a synthesizable, parameterized, open-source out-of-order RISC-V processor core written in Chisel. It delivers high-performance single-thread execution rivaling commercial ARM Cortex-A9/A15 and Intel Skylake class microarchitectures while maintaining full compatibility with the open RISC-V ecosystem.

## Microarchitecture Highlights
- **Pipeline Depth:** 7 to 10 stages from instruction fetch to writeback and commit.
- **Configurable Superscalar Width:** Parameterized from 1-wide (SmallBOOM), 2-wide (MediumBOOM), 3-wide (LargeBOOM), to 4-wide decode/rename/dispatch (MegaBOOM).
- **Execution Units:** Decoupled Issue Queues feeding separate Integer ALUs, Pipelined Multipliers, Floating-Point Units (FPUs), and Address Generation Units (AGUs).
- **Out-of-Order Renaming:** Explicit Register Renaming with Physical Register Files (PRF) supporting up to 128 physical integer and floating-point registers.
- **Speculative Memory Subsystem:** Deep Reorder Buffer (ROB) supporting up to 128 in-flight instructions, combined with a non-blocking Load-Store Queue (LSQ) with dynamic memory disambiguation and store-to-load forwarding.
- **Branch Prediction:** State-of-the-art TAGE-SC branch predictor, Branch Target Buffer (BTB), and Return Address Stack (RAS) minimizing pipeline flushes.

## Coherence & System Integration
- **Interconnect:** Native TileLink-C (TL-C) coherent cache interface integrating with Rocket Chip / Chipyard coherent crossbars and shared L2 caches.
- **Silicon Proven:** Fabricated across multiple TSMC 16nm and 28nm research test chips under the UC Berkeley ADEPT laboratory.
`;

