  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- SECTION 1: ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS & THE MEMORY WALL -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <section id="core-foundations" class="content-block">
    <div class="block-header-wrap">
      <div class="badge badge-primary">&sect; 1. ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS: CORE ANATOMY &amp; THE MEMORY WALL</div>
      <h2 class="block-title">The Anatomy of an Application Core &amp; Conquering the Memory Wall</h2>
      <p class="block-lead">
        Before evaluating specific 64-bit processors or classifying them into microarchitectural archetypes, we must establish the physical foundations of high-performance computing: <strong>what an application-class RISC-V core is constructed from</strong>, and <strong>how modern open architectures bridge the 100&times; speed disparity between gigahertz silicon execution units and nanosecond DRAM</strong>.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- 1.1 THE SEVEN UNIVERSAL SUBSYSTEMS -->
    <div class="concept-step-card" style="margin-bottom: 2rem;">
      <div class="step-card-header">
        <span class="step-tag badge-ink">1.1 THE SEVEN UNIVERSAL SUBSYSTEMS</span>
        <h3 class="step-card-title">Anatomy of an Application-Class 64-Bit RISC-V Core</h3>
      </div>
      <p class="step-card-lead">
        In deeply embedded microcontroller cores, instruction throughput is primarily bounded by ALU critical paths and simple branch bubble penalties. In Category 2 Application Cores, the fundamental engineering barrier shifts entirely to the <strong>Memory Wall</strong>: while 64-bit ALUs and FPU execution pipelines operate at gigahertz frequencies (&lt;1.0 ns clock period), external DDR DRAM memory takes 50 to 100 ns (100 to 200 clock cycles) to respond. Furthermore, running multi-user operating systems (such as Linux 6.1) mandates hardware-enforced privilege rings, dynamic virtual memory translation, and cache coherence.
        Every evaluated 64-bit application core (from 135 kGE in-order cores up to 420 kGE out-of-order superscalar processors) implements seven coordinated hardware primitives to conquer memory latency and sustain instruction flow:
      </p>

