    <div id="archetype-3-deepdive" class="concept-stream" style="margin-top: 2.5rem;">
      <div class="concept-step-card archetype-lime-border">
        <div class="step-card-header">
          <span class="step-tag badge-lime">2.3 ARCHITECTURAL DEEP-DIVE</span>
          <h3 class="step-card-title">Archetype 3: Modular Coherent Multi-Core Cluster Tile (BlackParrot)</h3>
        </div>
        <p class="step-card-lead">
          Co-developed by the University of Washington and the Bespoke Silicon Group, <strong>BlackParrot</strong> was engineered from inception to solve the multi-core scaling crisis. Rather than treating multi-core clustering as an afterthought glued together with ad-hoc interconnects, BlackParrot treats the processor core as a repeatable, composable "cluster tile" incorporating formal directory-based MESI cache coherence and latency-insensitive credit-based NoC packet routing.
        </p>

        <!-- 2.3.1 BlackParrot Technical Breakdown -->
        <div class="sub-topic-card">
          <div class="sub-topic-head">
            <div class="sub-topic-title-group">
              <span class="pill-badge badge-lime">CORE 3</span>
              <h4 class="sub-topic-title">BlackParrot &bull; Modular Coherent Multi-Core Tile</h4>
            </div>
            <span class="sub-topic-spec-badge">~165 kGE / Tile &bull; SystemVerilog</span>
          </div>
          <p>
            BlackParrot revolutionizes warehouse-scale processor scaling through modular elasticity and formal coherence:
          </p>
          <ul class="concept-list">
            <li><strong>Modular 3-Partition Architecture:</strong> The processor is partitioned into three decoupled modules: Front-End (FE: branch prediction, instruction fetch, ITLB), Back-End (BE: decode, scoreboard issue, integer ALU, FPU), and Memory-End (ME: DTLB, L1-D cache, MSHRs, and Coherence Cache Engine).</li>
            <li><strong>Latency-Insensitive Design (LID):</strong> Communication across FE, BE, and ME occurs via standardized credit-based elastic FIFOs. Long wire delays and clock skews between physical blocks do not cause timing hazards or require pipeline stalls, enabling clean physical place-and-route across massive silicon dies.</li>
            <li><strong>BedRock Directory-Based MESI Coherence:</strong> Every BlackParrot tile contains a Coherence Cache Engine (CCE) that communicates with a local L2 cache slice and distributed directory tags. The BedRock protocol formally guarantees race-free cache coherence across 1 to 64 cores without broadcast storms.</li>
            <li><strong>Integrated 2D-Mesh Network-on-Chip (NoC):</strong> Each tile embeds a 5-port packetized wormhole router (North, South, East, West, and Local Core). Cores can be tiled directly in a 2D mesh array, scaling computing throughput linearly with silicon area.</li>
            <li><strong>Silicon Workload &amp; Target Application:</strong> Consumes ~165 kGE per tile. It is the premier open-source solution for domain-specific accelerator hosts, massively parallel multi-core server processors, and tiled datacenter compute arrays.</li>
          </ul>

