  <!-- SECTION 4: MICROARCHITECTURAL DIFFERENCES & DIVERSITY       -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <section id="microarchitectural-differences" class="content-block">
    <div class="block-header-wrap">
      <div class="badge badge-coral">&sect; 4. MICROARCHITECTURAL DIVERSITY</div>
      <h2 class="block-title">Cross-Core Differences: Engineering Trade-Offs &amp; Diversity</h2>
      <p class="block-lead">
        While strict RV64GC compliance guarantees software portability, the underlying microarchitectural implementations diverge dramatically. Engineering teams make radically different silicon trade-offs across issue width, memory concurrency (MSHRs), system interconnects, and speculative branch prediction to satisfy competing power, area, and throughput targets.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Differences Dimension Cards -->
    <div class="diff-cards-deck">
      <!-- Difference 1: Issue Width & Pipeline Scheduling -->
      <div class="diff-spec-card diff-hazards">
        <div class="diff-spec-head">
          <span class="diff-tag badge-coral">PIPELINE SCHEDULING</span>
          <h4>Issue Width &amp; Hazard Resolution: In-Order vs. Scoreboard vs. Out-of-Order PRF</h4>
        </div>
        <p class="diff-lead">
          The instruction dispatch and dependency-tracking strategy dictates how the processor handles data hazards and long-latency multicycle operations:
        </p>
        <div class="diff-points-stack">
          <div class="diff-point-row">
            <span class="diff-point-title">5-Stage Rigid In-Order (Rocket Chip)</span>
            <span class="diff-point-text">Single-issue in-order dispatch. Stalls the entire front-end on multicycle operations (FPU division, unaligned memory accesses, or DRAM cache misses). Eliminates register rename logic and reservation stations, achieving the smallest gate count (~135 kGE) and lowest dynamic power.</span>
          </div>
          <div class="diff-point-row">
            <span class="diff-point-title">6-Stage Decoupled Scoreboard (CVA6 / Ariane)</span>
            <span class="diff-point-text">Decouples instruction issue from execution via a central scoreboard table. Independent 1-cycle integer ALU instructions can bypass long-latency operations (division or floating-point FMA), while results commit to architectural state strictly in program order to guarantee precise traps.</span>
          </div>
          <div class="diff-point-row">
            <span class="diff-point-title">Modular Elastic Scoreboard (BlackParrot)</span>
            <span class="diff-point-text">Decoupled Front-End (FE) and Back-End (BE) connected by credit-based elastic FIFOs. Features scoreboard-based dependency resolution with dual issue channels and distributed trap handling across modular partitions.</span>
          </div>
          <div class="diff-point-row">
            <span class="diff-point-title">3-Wide Out-of-Order with Unified PRF (SonicBOOM)</span>
            <span class="diff-point-text">Fetches 4 instructions and renames/dispatches 3 uOPs per clock cycle. Maps 32 architectural registers onto a 128-entry Physical Register File (PRF), completely eliminating false WAR/WAW hazards. Instructions execute speculatively out-of-order via distributed reservation stations and commit through a 128-entry circular Reorder Buffer (RoB).</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Difference 2: Memory Wall & MSHR Concurrency -->
      <div class="diff-spec-card diff-math">
        <div class="diff-spec-head">
          <span class="diff-tag badge-yellow">MEMORY WALL CONCURRENCY</span>
          <h4>Cache Miss Concurrency: Blocking vs. Non-Blocking MSHR Pools</h4>
        </div>
        <p class="diff-lead">
          A major bottleneck in 64-bit application computing is the Memory Wall: servicing a DRAM line fill takes 50 to 100 clock cycles. Cores diverge fundamentally in how they handle memory latency:
        </p>
        <div class="diff-points-stack">
          <div class="diff-point-row">
            <span class="diff-point-title">Blocking Single-Miss Cache (Rocket Chip)</span>
            <span class="diff-point-text">4-way set-associative VIPT cache without MSHRs. On a cache miss, the pipeline freezes completely until the TileLink-UH bus refills the entire 64-byte line from DRAM. Instantaneous CPI degrades to 50&ndash;100 cycles during DRAM latency.</span>
          </div>
          <div class="diff-point-row">
            <span class="diff-point-title">Non-Blocking Hit-Under-Miss (BlackParrot)</span>
            <span class="diff-point-text">The L1 Data Cache incorporates dedicated Miss Status Holding Registers. Subsequent load and store instructions that hit in L1-D continue executing uninterrupted while a prior cache miss is being serviced across the BedRock interconnect.</span>
          </div>
          <div class="diff-point-row">
            <span class="diff-point-title">8-Entry Concurrent MSHR Pool (SonicBOOM)</span>
            <span class="diff-point-text">Features an 8-entry MSHR pool supporting both "hits-under-miss" and "misses-under-miss". Sustains up to 8 concurrent outstanding 64-byte DRAM line requests simultaneously. Combined with a 16-entry Load Queue (LDQ) and 16-entry Store Queue (STQ) providing speculative load bypassing and dynamic store-to-load forwarding.</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Difference 3: System Interconnect & Coherence Fabric -->
      <div class="diff-spec-card diff-memory">
        <div class="diff-spec-head">
          <span class="diff-tag badge-teal">SYSTEM INTERCONNECT</span>
          <h4>System Bus Interfaces &amp; Multi-Core Coherence Fabrics</h4>
        </div>
        <p class="diff-lead">
          Interconnect architecture dictates how easily an application core integrates into industrial SoC platforms and how effectively it scales across multi-core cluster configurations:
        </p>
        <div class="diff-points-stack">
          <div class="diff-point-row">
            <span class="diff-point-title">TileLink-UH 64b (Rocket Chip)</span>
            <span class="diff-point-text">Decoupled open-source bus protocol with independent Channel A (requests) and Channel D (responses). Highly optimized for low-gate-count single-core SoCs, but multi-core coherence requires adding a centralized TileLink Broadcast Hub or complex L2 directory controller.</span>
          </div>
          <div class="diff-point-row">
            <span class="diff-point-title">Standard Industrial AXI4-64b (CVA6 / Ariane)</span>
            <span class="diff-point-text">Full ARM AMBA AXI4 master interface with 5 independent channels (AW, W, B, AR, R). Provides native plug-and-play compatibility with commercial ASIC memory controllers, PCIe root complexes, and standard FPGA IP blocks without bridge translation logic.</span>
          </div>
          <div class="diff-point-row">
            <span class="diff-point-title">BedRock Directory MESI &amp; 2D-Mesh NoC (BlackParrot)</span>
            <span class="diff-point-text">Embeds an on-chip Coherence Cache Engine (CCE) running the formal BedRock directory-based MESI protocol over credit-based packet FIFOs. Includes an integrated 5-port 2D-mesh packetized wormhole router directly inside each tile, supporting linear multi-core scaling up to 64 cores without broadcast storms.</span>
          </div>
          <div class="diff-point-row">
            <span class="diff-point-title">Multi-Channel TileLink-C Coherent Bus (SonicBOOM)</span>
            <span class="diff-point-text">Full 5-channel TileLink Coherent (TL-C) fabric with independent acquire, probe, and grant channels, interfacing with an interleaved multi-bank shared L2 cache and hardware directory snooper for high-bandwidth superscalar memory pipelines.</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Difference 4: Branch Prediction & Speculative Window -->
      <div class="diff-spec-card diff-security">
        <div class="diff-spec-head">
          <span class="diff-tag badge-purple">BRANCH SPECULATION</span>
          <h4>Dynamic Branch Predictors &amp; Speculative Recovery Windows</h4>
        </div>
        <p class="diff-lead">
          As pipeline depth increases, the penalty for branch mispredictions escalates from 2 cycles to over 10 cycles, driving increasingly sophisticated branch prediction hardware:
        </p>
        <div class="diff-points-stack">
          <div class="diff-point-row">
            <span class="diff-point-title">28-Entry BTB + 2-Bit BHT (Rocket Chip)</span>
            <span class="diff-point-text">Simple Branch Target Buffer (28 entries) with a 2-bit saturating counter array. Branches resolve in the EX stage with a modest 2-cycle bubble penalty on mispredicts. Minimal silicon area footprint (~2.5 kGE).</span>
          </div>
          <div class="diff-point-row">
            <span class="diff-point-title">Two-Level BHT + BTB + 4-Entry RAS (CVA6 / Ariane)</span>
            <span class="diff-point-text">Combines a 2-level Branch History Table (BHT), Branch Target Buffer, and 4-entry Return Address Stack (RAS). Bounding mispredict penalties strictly to 3 clock cycles.</span>
          </div>
          <div class="diff-point-row">
            <span class="diff-point-title">Modular Elastic BPU (BlackParrot)</span>
            <span class="diff-point-text">Decoupled branch prediction engine located in the Front-End partition, feeding an elastic instruction queue to absorb redirect latency from the execution back-end.</span>
          </div>
          <div class="diff-point-row">
            <span class="diff-point-title">Advanced TAGE Predictor + 32-Entry RAS + FTQ (SonicBOOM)</span>
            <span class="diff-point-text">Deploys an industry-grade TAgged GEometric history length (TAGE) branch predictor with multi-component global history tables, Next-Line Predictor (NLP), 32-entry RAS, and a 16-entry Fetch Target Queue (FTQ). Achieves &gt;96% accuracy on complex branch-heavy Linux workloads, avoiding devastating 8-to-10 cycle pipeline flushes.</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

